## Document Object Model (DOM)

### Definition

**DOM (Document Object Model)** is a programming interface that represents an HTML or XML document as a **tree of objects or nodes**.

It allows JavaScript to **access, modify, add, or delete** HTML elements, attributes, and content dynamically.

In simple words, **DOM converts an HTML document into a tree structure**, where every HTML element is represented as a node.

### DOM Tree of an HTML Document

Consider the following HTML:

```html
<html>
    <head>
        <title>My Page</title>
    </head>
    <body>
        <h1>Hello</h1>
        <p>Welcome to HTML</p>
    </body>
</html>
```

The DOM tree can be represented as:

```text
                    Document
                       |
                     <html>
                    /      \
                 <head>    <body>
                   |       /    \
                <title>   <h1>   <p>
                   |       |      |
              "My Page"  "Hello" "Welcome to HTML"
```

### Main Nodes in the DOM Tree

1. **Document Node** – Represents the complete HTML document.
2. **Element Node** – Represents HTML elements such as `<html>`, `<body>`, `<p>`, etc.
3. **Attribute Node** – Represents attributes such as `id`, `class`, and `href`.
4. **Text Node** – Represents the text contained inside HTML elements.

### Uses of DOM

* It allows JavaScript to **change HTML content**.
* It can **change CSS styles** dynamically.
* It can **add or remove HTML elements**.
* It allows JavaScript to respond to **events**, such as clicks and mouse movements.

### Conclusion

The DOM provides a **tree-like representation of an HTML document**. JavaScript uses the DOM to interact with and dynamically modify the structure, content, and style of a web page.
