## WML Program to Display Username and Password

### Assumption

We assume that a WAP-enabled mobile device is used to enter a **username and password**. The form contains two input fields and a submit button.

### WML Program

```xml
<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!DOCTYPE wml PUBLIC "-//WAPFORUM//DTD WML 1.1//EN"
"http://www.wapforum.org/DTD/wml_1.1.xml">

<wml>
    <card id="login" title="Login">

        <p>
            Username:
            <input name="username" type="text"/>
            <br/>

            Password:
            <input name="password" type="password"/>
            <br/>

            <anchor>
                Login
                <go href="login.wml" method="post">
                    <postfield name="username" value="$(username)"/>
                    <postfield name="password" value="$(password)"/>
                </go>
            </anchor>
        </p>

    </card>
</wml>
```

### Explanation

1. `<?xml version="1.0"?>` declares the XML version.
2. `<wml>` is the root element of a WML document.
3. `<card>` defines a screen or page displayed on the WAP device.
4. `<input type="text">` is used to enter the **username**.
5. `<input type="password">` is used to enter the **password**. The password is normally hidden while typing.
6. `<anchor>` creates a link/action for the user.
7. `<go>` specifies the action to be performed when the user selects **Login**.
8. `<postfield>` sends the username and password to the specified server.

### Expected Output

```text
-------------------------
       LOGIN
-------------------------
Username: [___________]

Password: [___________]

        [ Login ]
-------------------------
```

### Conclusion

WML provides form controls such as `<input>` for accepting user information on **WAP-enabled mobile devices**. The above program creates a simple login form for entering a username and password.
